From: NAPI loop <noreply@local>
Date: Fri, 24 Oct 2026 10:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: napi-c: force PHY 100/full (no autoneg) for fast loopdetect

The passive single-port loopback makes the PHY autonegotiate against its
own reflected link pulses, which takes ~13s to bring the link up on a cold
boot; patch 1134 worked around that by raising CONFIG_PHY_ANEG_TIMEOUT to
20000, at the cost of every no-loopback (SD) cold boot waiting out that
~20s timeout before loopdetect gives up.

Force the onboard Designware PHY to a fixed 100BASE-TX full-duplex link
with autonegotiation disabled (new CONFIG_NAPI_PHY_FORCE_100FD):

  * dw_phy_init(): set autoneg=disable, speed=100, duplex=full before
    phy_config(), so genphy_config_aneg() takes the genphy_setup_forced()
    path and writes a forced BMCR instead of starting autoneg.
  * designware_eth_init(): since genphy_update_link() returns immediately
    in forced mode, poll the link for up to 3s after phy_startup() so a
    loopback that is coming up is still caught.

Result: a loopback links in ~1-2s (link detected on signal, no autoneg
handshake) and no-loopback is detected in ~3s instead of ~20s - the delay
is cut in both directions. If the PHY somehow fails to link in forced mode
the behaviour falls back to the autoneg path bounded by PHY_ANEG_TIMEOUT,
so this is a strict improvement with no regression.
---
--- a/drivers/net/designware.c	2026-10-24 10:24:09.613279344 +0000
+++ b/drivers/net/designware.c	2026-10-24 10:24:23.561364609 +0000
@@ -431,6 +431,22 @@
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * In forced (no-autoneg) mode genphy_update_link() returns at once, so
+	 * the link may still be down on the first read while a passive
+	 * loopback comes up. Poll briefly for the link instead of giving up;
+	 * if no loopback is present it stays down and we bail out quickly.
+	 */
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NAPI_PHY_FORCE_100FD) && !priv->phydev->link) {
+		int wait_ms;
+
+		for (wait_ms = 0; wait_ms < 3000 && !priv->phydev->link;
+		     wait_ms += 100) {
+			mdelay(100);
+			genphy_update_link(priv->phydev);
+		}
+	}
+
 	ret = dw_adjust_link(priv, mac_p, priv->phydev);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
@@ -617,6 +633,22 @@
 	phydev->advertising = phydev->supported;
 
 	priv->phydev = phydev;
+
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NAPI_PHY_FORCE_100FD)) {
+		/*
+		 * Force a fixed 100BASE-TX full-duplex link with autoneg
+		 * disabled. A passive single-port loopback then links in
+		 * ~1-2s (no autoneg handshake against its own reflected link
+		 * pulses, which otherwise takes ~13s), and when no loopback is
+		 * present the link stays down and is detected at once instead
+		 * of waiting out CONFIG_PHY_ANEG_TIMEOUT. Used by the NAPI-C
+		 * 'loopdetect' boot selector.
+		 */
+		phydev->autoneg = AUTONEG_DISABLE;
+		phydev->speed = SPEED_100;
+		phydev->duplex = DUPLEX_FULL;
+	}
+
 	phy_config(phydev);
 
 	return 0;
--- a/drivers/net/Kconfig	2026-10-24 10:24:09.640912467 +0000
+++ b/drivers/net/Kconfig	2026-10-24 10:24:31.030095154 +0000
@@ -787,6 +787,17 @@
 	  This driver provides Rockchip SoCs network support based on the
 	  Synopsys Designware driver.
 
+config NAPI_PHY_FORCE_100FD
+	bool "NAPI-C: force onboard PHY to 100/full, no autoneg (fast loopdetect)"
+	depends on ETH_DESIGNWARE
+	help
+	  Force the Designware MAC's PHY to a fixed 100BASE-TX full-duplex link
+	  with autonegotiation disabled. A passive single-port loopback then
+	  links in ~1-2s instead of ~13s (no autoneg handshake), and when no
+	  loopback is present the link stays down and is detected at once
+	  instead of waiting out PHY_ANEG_TIMEOUT. Intended for the NAPI-C
+	  'loopdetect' boot selector.
+
 config RENESAS_ETHER_SWITCH
 	bool "Renesas Ethernet Switch support"
 	depends on DM_ETH && R8A779F0
--- a/configs/napi-c-rk3308_defconfig
+++ b/configs/napi-c-rk3308_defconfig
@@ -76,3 +76,4 @@
 CONFIG_USE_PREBOOT=y
 CONFIG_PREBOOT="loopdetect; if test \"${napi_loop}\" = 1; then echo NAPI loopback present: booting eMMC first; setenv boot_targets \"mmc0 mmc1 nvme scsi usb pxe dhcp spi\"; fi"
 CONFIG_PHY_ANEG_TIMEOUT=20000
+CONFIG_NAPI_PHY_FORCE_100FD=y
